public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated
public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-!
# The ideal sheaf `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` of Section 52.7, built from the data

[Stacks, Tags 0A0G and 0A0I] compare `Rf_*(K^∧)` with `(Rf_*K)^∧` along a morphism
`f : (Sh(𝒟), 𝒪') ⟶ (Sh(𝒞), 𝒪)`, the completion on the source being taken along the ideal sheaf
`ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` that the source *constructs* out of `ℐ`. The statements of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean` instead take two independent generating families
and the relation `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible` between the ideal
sheaves they generate, because on a general morphism of sites there is no transport of a global
section of `𝒪` to one of `𝒪'`: a global section in the sense of
`CategoryTheory.Presheaf.globalSections` is a compatible family over *every* object of the site,
while `f^♯` produces sections only over the objects in the image of `f.u`.

That obstruction disappears as soon as the target site has a terminal object `T` whose image
`f.u T` is terminal in the source site — which is the case for a morphism of ringed *spaces*,
where `T = ⊤` and `f.u ⊤ = f^{-1}⊤ = ⊤`, and hence for a morphism of schemes, the display's own
setting. There a global section is its value over `T`, and `t ↦ f^♯(t)` is the transport. This
file makes that transport, proves that the ideal sheaf generated by the transported family *is*
`f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, and reads the statements of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean` at
it.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.pushGlobalSection` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.pushGlobalSections`: the transport of a global section of `𝒪`,
  and of a finite family of them, to the source site, along a morphism of ringed sites carrying
  a terminal object to a terminal object.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.fSharpSection_map`: `f^♯` commutes with restriction.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.pushforwardIdealCompatible_generatedIdealSheaf`: for a sheaf of
  ideals generated by finitely many global sections of `𝒪`, the relation
  `PushforwardIdealCompatible` holds as soon as `f^♯` carries the *generators* into `ℐ'`. This
  is the step that needs the site structure: membership in `ℐ` is local, and `f.u` sends a
  covering sieve to a covering sieve because it is continuous.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.pushforwardIdealCompatible_pushGlobalSections`: the relation
  `PushforwardIdealCompatible f ℐ ℐ'` for `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_s)` and
  `ℐ' = (f^♯t_1, …, f^♯t_s)`, proved from `f` and `t`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.generatedIdealSheaf_pushGlobalSections_le` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.eq_generatedIdealSheaf_pushGlobalSections`: that ideal sheaf is
  the *smallest* sheaf of ideals of `𝒪'` receiving `f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ)`, and is therefore the
  `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` of Section 52.7 and nothing else.
* In the namespace `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite`,
  `isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_inverseImageIdealSheaf`,
  `derivedCompletionPushforwardComparisonInverseImageIdealSheaf`,
  `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf_of_isIso_map_unit` and
  `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete`:
  the four statements of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean` at `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_s)`
  and `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, so depending on `f`, on the two terminal objects and on `t` only.
* In each of the namespaces `Stacks.RingedSpace` and `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme`,
  `isDerivedComplete_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_inverseImageIdealSheaf` and
  `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf_of_isDerivedComplete`:
  the same at a morphism of ringed spaces and at a morphism of schemes, where the two
  terminality inputs are discharged by `CategoryTheory.Limits.isTerminalTop` on the site of
  opens.

## What is covered of the two tags, and what is not

Everything below depends on a morphism, the terminal objects of the two sites and a finite
family of global sections of `𝒪`, and on nothing else. Both derived completion functors are
produced from those generators, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf`, so no
part of [Stacks, Tag 099F] is an input; and the second sheaf of ideals is built rather than
received, so the relation between the two ideals that
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean` takes in is proved here. One restriction on the
data remains; the object is unrestricted:

*The object.* The invertibility of the comparison map for an **arbitrary** `K` is the assertion
of [Stacks, Tag 0A0G], and at this very pair of sheaves of ideals it is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf`
of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionCanonical.lean`, which runs the source's computation in the
Koszul Hom tower and then transports its conclusion onto the canonical map. The statements below
are the ones that need no such computation: they restrict `K` to the derived-complete objects,
or make the invertibility of `Rf_*(K ⟶ K^∧)` the criterion to be fed, and in exchange they ask
nothing of the two sheaves of ideals beyond the source's relation between them.

*The data.* A finite-type sheaf of ideals is only **locally** generated by finitely many
sections, while `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_s)` asks for generators on the site itself. That restriction is
lifted in `PushforwardCommutesCompletionFiniteType.lean`, where the derived completion functors
come from [Stacks, Tag 099F] at a sheaf of ideals of finite type — the instance
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` of `FiniteTypeReflector.lean`
— and the display is proved at a morphism of ringed spaces and at a morphism of schemes for two
sheaves of ideals of finite type. The second sheaf of ideals is a parameter there rather than
built from `ℐ`; the one built here is of finite type, by
`Stacks.RingedSpace.isFiniteType_generatedIdealSheaf` of that file, so the statements here are
instances of the ones there.

## Implementation notes

*Where the site structure enters.* `f^♯` carries each generator of `ℐ` into `ℐ'` by hypothesis,
but membership in `ℐ = (t_1, …, t_s)` is *local*: a section of `ℐ` over `W` is only locally on
`W` an `𝒪`-linear combination of the `t_i`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.mem_generatedIdealSheaf_iff`). So
`pushforwardIdealCompatible_generatedIdealSheaf` pushes that covering sieve of `W` forward to a
covering sieve of `f.u W` — which is where continuity of `f.u` is used, through
`CategoryTheory.CoverPreserving.of_isContinuous` — observes that `f^♯(s)` restricts into `ℐ'`
along each of its arrows, by naturality of `f^♯` and because `ℐ'` is closed under
multiplication by sections of `𝒪'`, and concludes because `ℐ'` is a sheaf of ideals.

*Why the ideal sheaf on the source is the source's own.* `generatedIdealSheaf (pushGlobalSections
f hT' t)` contains `f^♯(s)` for every section `s` of `ℐ` over every object, by
`pushforwardIdealCompatible_pushGlobalSections`, and by
`generatedIdealSheaf_pushGlobalSections_le` it is contained in every sheaf of ideals that does.
Those two properties are the definition of `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`, so
`eq_generatedIdealSheaf_pushGlobalSections` identifies any object with them with this one. The
leastness is where terminality is used a second time: the transported generators are the
restrictions, from the terminal object, of the sections `f^♯(t_i)` living over `f.u T`, which is
an object in the image of `f.u` and so one at which the defining property of `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`
applies.

*Two families or one.* The statements of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean` keep the
two generating families independent, which is strictly more general as a statement about two
sheaves of ideals; the ones here are the special case `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` of them, which is the
case Section 52.7 is about. Neither supersedes the other and the registrations of both stand.

## References

